]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} [:a {:href "/athletes/69977"} "Petra Felke"] " (GER) was the defending champion but was not the dominant thrower she had been in 1988. She had finished second at the 1991 World Championships behind " [:a {:href "/athletes/66472"} "Xu Demei"] " (CHN), while the 1990 European Champion was Finland’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/67471"} "Päivi Alafrantti"] ". But the best throwers in 1992 had been Belarussian " [:a {:href "/athletes/65415"} "Nataliya Shikolenko"] " and Norway’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/74086"} "Trine Hattestad"] ", and they led the qualifiers. Shikolenko took the lead in the final with 68.26 (223-11½) in the first round. Round one produced all the medalists as " [:a {:href "/athletes/70086"} "Silke Renk"] " (GER) threw 67.24 (220-7¼) and " [:a {:href "/athletes/69981"} "Karen Forkel"] ", eventual bronze medalist, threw 65.02 (213-4), and nobody would surpass those marks except the leaders. The standings held up through five rounds with Shikolenko leading Renk, who then won the gold medal with 68.34 (224-2½) on her final toss."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Number"] [:th {} "Competitor"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "Qualifying"] [:th {} "Final"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {:class "bib"} "841"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/70086"} "Silke Renk"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/GER"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/GER.png"}] "GER"]] [:td {} "65.38 (4)"] [:td {} "68.34 (1)"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Gold"} "Gold"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "2"] [:td {:class "bib"} "495"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/65415"} "Natallia Shykalenka"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/EUN"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/EUN.png"}]